            <ArticleTitle>Management of large coronary dissection after STAR.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>58-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.carrev.2013.07.001</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1553-8389(13)00107-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The guided-STAR technique is an alternative anterograde approach for treatment of coronary chronic total occlusion (CTO) and it is usually followed by implantation of multiple stents. We describe a case of residual long coronary dissection left unstented after guided-STAR, with good results at 2months follow-up. This case emphasizes the fact that sometimes even the longest coronary dissections can be left unstented especially in the contest of a CTO.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2014.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
